      <article class="guide-card">
        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync resource</div>
        <h2>Use it to make the next step clearer</h2>
        <p class="intro">Lead routing is a sequence of decisions: is the record valid, is there a clear owner, is the prospect a fit, and how quickly should someone respond? This framework gives your team a repeatable way to design that sequence before turning it into workflows.</p>
        <section><h3>Decision 1: Is the record ready to route?</h3><p>Do not route incomplete or unsafe records into a sales queue. Confirm the required contact details, consent state, source, and key context before creating an assignment.</p><ul><li>Check email validity, country, source, and consent.</li><li>Identify existing contacts, companies, and owners before creating duplicates.</li><li>Send incomplete records to a data-quality or nurture path.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Decision 2: Who should own it?</h3><p>Apply rules in a deliberate order. Territory, named-account ownership, product, language, and customer status usually take priority over a generic round-robin rule.</p><ul><li>Define the priority order for territory and account ownership.</li><li>Use fit and intent signals to separate qualified from nurture paths.</li><li>Create a fallback owner and a visible no-owner queue.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Decision 3: What happens if nobody responds?</h3><p>Every route needs a service level and recovery path. Stamp assignment and response times so the system can escalate a missed handoff instead of allowing a qualified lead to disappear.</p><ul><li>Set response expectations by lead type and priority.</li><li>Notify, reassign, or escalate before the SLA expires.</li><li>Review unworked, recycled, and rejected records every business day.</li></ul></section>
        <section>
          <h3>Resource checklist</h3>
          <p>Use these checkpoints to turn the template into a working process.</p>
          <div class="guide-checklist"><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Required fields defined</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Duplicate check included</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Routing priority documented</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Fallback owner assigned</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>SLA timers configured</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Escalation queue monitored</div></div></div>
        </section>
        <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> A decision tree makes routing transparent. When the team can see why a lead went somewhere, they can improve the rules instead of guessing at the outcome.</p></div>
